lines=['# Integrated native/main benchmark evidence','',f'Pinned commit: `{a["commit"]}`. Node remains the default npm CLI; native is an experimental flatten, ASCII-insensitive literal-search, and ordered-unflatten subset. The unmerged shell usability builder is excluded.','', '## Decision evidence','', 'On the 1000 MiB event fixture, native flatten was effectively tied with fastgron (3.811 vs 3.824 s) while using about 1.69 vs 1899 MiB RSS. Native search took 2.395 s, Node 4.800 s, and fastgron 3.031 s. Ordered native unflatten (6.204 s) and Node (6.310 s) were close; native used about 1.75 MiB RSS versus Node 101 MiB and fastgron 7578 MiB. The strongest evidence is lower memory and startup/cap cost, plus search throughput on this fixture. This does not justify replacing the broader default Node CLI or claiming a general speed lead over fastgron.','', '## Validation','', '26 native release tests passed. All three compatibility suites passed: 192 flatten byte comparisons, 432 search byte/status comparisons, and 159 ordered-unflatten comparisons. See retained logs for malformed-input, caps, depth and fallback rejection checks.','', 'All outputs were streamed to SHA256 sinks. Native and Node unflatten are compared against the original compact fixture plus LF. Fastgron serializer whitespace and object-key order differ; supplemental flatten → unflatten → native reflatten pipelines check reconstructed values, key order and raw number lexemes without keeping giant output copies. Order-sensitive reflatten hashes therefore differ for fastgron event records; those mismatches are not alone evidence of wrong values. A separate bounded, record-by-record JSON decoder sorts object keys and hashes canonical records to check semantic equality at every size; this event-only check uses ordinary finite numbers, while raw numeric lexemes have a separate exact test. Validation is recorded per sample in validation.json; failures are not counted as passing equality checks.','', '## Method and limits','', 'Three serial samples per measurement, tool order rotated each round, input cache warmed before each sample. Exact commands and all samples are in evidence.json and supplement.json. Wall time uses a monotonic clock around the full CLI process and streaming sink; /usr/bin/time -l logs retain real/user/system time and maximum RSS. Pipeline RSS is the largest individual child; a separate 100 ms process-tree sum enforces the 8 GiB guard. Timeout is 120 seconds per sample. Very brief peaks can fall between guard polls. SHA256 sink overhead and pipe backpressure are included; these are full CLI measurements, not internal parser timings or /dev/null throughput.','', 'Apple M5 Pro, 48 GiB RAM, macOS shared host. Other active processes, including a separate fastgron process, were observed during this run; host snapshots and per-sample load are retained. Small differences and local winners are not isolated-host findings.','', 'The repository bench-compare eventRecord generator is copied verbatim into generate.mjs, seed 42, including Unicode strings. Its 2,000-record batches overshoot target sizes; exact byte sizes and hashes below are authoritative. One common native-generated gron file was retained per size while timing unflatten, then removed; its hash and bytes remain in the receipt. The four JSON fixtures remain available.','', 'jq --stream -c . emits parser events, including container-end events. It is not gron flattening and is not ranked as an equivalent implementation. Shell caps use head/grep and last-command status, so they describe consumer cancellation and may read/parse more than native/Node explicit caps; stderr is retained. Supplemental validation pipeline timing includes two native flatten passes and must not be read as standalone unflatten performance.','', '## Fixtures','', '| Target MiB | Actual bytes | Search matches | SHA256 |','|---:|---:|---:|---|']
